{{Short description|Average measure of the efficiency of production}}
{{About|the economic and business concept}}
{{Economics sidebar}}

'''Productivity''' is the [[efficiency]] of [[production (economics)|production]] of [[goods]] or [[Service (economics)|services]] expressed by some measure. Measurements of productivity are often expressed as a ratio of an aggregate [[output (economics)|output]] to a single [[input (economic)|input]] or an aggregate input used in a production process, i.e. output per unit of input, typically over a specific period of time.<ref>{{Cite book |editor-last=Kaliski |editor-first=Burton S.|title=Encyclopedia of busine$$ and finance|date=2001|publisher=Macmillan Reference USA |isbn=0028650654|location=New York|oclc=45403115}}</ref> The most common example is the (aggregate) [[labour productivity]] measure, one example of which is [[GDP]] per worker. There are many different definitions of productivity (including those that are not defined as ratios of output to input) and the choice among them depends on the purpose of the productivity measurement and data availability. The key source of difference between various productivity measures is also usually related (directly or indirectly) to how the outputs and the inputs are aggregated to obtain such a ratio-type measure of productivity.<ref name="assets.cambridge.org">{{cite book|last1=Sickles|first1=Robin|author1-link=Robin Sickles|last2=Zelenyuk|first2=Valentin|year=2019|title=Measurement of Productivity and Efficiency: Theory and Practice|location=Cambridge|publisher=Cambridge University Press|doi=10.1017/9781139565981|isbn=9781139565981|s2cid=155765388 }}</ref>

Productivity is a crucial factor in the production performance of firms and nations. Increasing national productivity can raise living standards because [[wage growth]] improves people's ability to purchase goods and services, enjoy leisure, improve housing, and education and contribute to social and environmental programs. Productivity growth can also help businesses to be more profitable.<ref>{{harvnb|Courbois|Temple|1975}}, {{harvnb|Gollop|1979}}, {{harvnb|Kurosawa|1975}}, {{harvnb|Pineda|1990}}, {{harvnb|Saari|2006}}, Hitt and Brynjolfsson 1996,{{incomplete short citation|date=August 2022}} {{harvnb|Sickles|Zelenyuk|2019}}</ref>

==Partial productivity==
{{Main|Partial productivity}}

Productivity measures that use one class of inputs or [[Factors of production|factors]], but not multiple factors, are called partial productivities.<ref name="Sumanth">{{cite book|last1=Sumanth|first1=David J.|title=Total Productivity Management (TPmgt): A Systemic and Quantitative Approach to Compete in Quality, Price and Time|publisher=CRC Press|isbn=9781574440577|page=5|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=mLAv09ocvTsC&q=%22Total+productivity%22&pg=PA5|access-date=11 March 2017|language=en|date=1997-10-27}}</ref> In practice, measurement in production means measures of partial productivity. Interpreted correctly, these components are indicative of productivity development, and approximate the efficiency with which inputs are used in an economy to produce goods and services. However, productivity is only measured partially&nbsp;– or approximately. In a way, the measurements are defective because they do not measure everything, but it is possible to interpret correctly the results of partial productivity and to benefit from them in practical situations. At the company level, typical partial productivity measures are such things as worker hours, materials or energy used per unit of production.<ref name="Sumanth"/>

Before the widespread use of computer networks, partial productivity was tracked in tabular form and with hand-drawn graphs. [[Tabulating machine]]s for data processing began being widely used in the 1920s and 1930s and remained in use until mainframe computers became widespread in the late 1960s through the 1970s. By the late 1970s inexpensive computers allowed industrial operations to perform [[process control]] and track productivity. Today data collection is largely computerized and almost any variable can be viewed graphically in real time or retrieved for selected time periods.

===Labour productivity===
{{See also|Workforce productivity|List of countries by labour productivity}}[[File:Labour productivity levels in europe.svg|thumb|Labour productivity levels in 2012 in Europe. [[OECD]]]]
[[File:OECD Productivity levels 2007.svg|thumb|500px|Comparison of average labour productivity levels between the [[OECD]] member states. Productivity is measured as GDP per hour worked. Blue bars = higher than OECD-average productivity. Yellow bars = lower than average.]]
In macroeconomics, a common partial productivity measure is [[labour productivity]]. Labour productivity is a revealing indicator of several economic indicators as it offers a dynamic measure of [[economic growth]], [[competitiveness]], and living standards within an economy.{{Citation needed|date=July 2023}} It is the measure of labour productivity (and all that this measure takes into account) which helps explain the principal economic foundations that are necessary for both economic growth and social development. In general labour productivity is equal to the ratio between a measure of output volume (gross domestic product or gross value added) and a measure of input use (the total number of hours worked or total employment).{{Citation needed|date=July 2023}}

:<math>\text{labour productivity} = \frac{\text{output volume}}{\text{labor input use}}</math>

The output measure is typically net output, more specifically the [[value added]] by the process under consideration, i.e. the value of outputs minus the value of intermediate inputs. This is done in order to avoid double-counting when an output of one firm is used as an input by another in the same measurement.<ref name="BLS"/> In macroeconomics the most well-known and used measure of value-added is the [[gross domestic product]] or GDP. Increases in it are widely used as a measure of the economic growth of nations and industries. GDP is the income available for paying capital costs, labor compensation, taxes and profits.{{sfn|OECD|2008|loc=11}} Some economists instead use [[gross value added]] (GVA); there is normally a strong correlation between GDP and GVA.{{sfn|Freeman|2008|loc=5}}

The measure of input use reflects the time, effort and skills of the workforce. The denominator of the ratio of labour productivity, the input measure is the most important factor that influences the measure of labour productivity. Labour input is measured either by the total number of hours worked of all persons employed or total employment (head count).{{sfn|Freeman|2008|loc=5}} There are both advantages and disadvantages associated with the different input measures that are used in the calculation of labour productivity. It is generally accepted that the total number of hours worked is the most appropriate measure of labour input because a simple headcount of employed persons can hide changes in average hours worked and has difficulties accounting for variations in work such as a [[part-time contract]], [[paid leave]], [[overtime]], or shifts in normal hours. However, the quality of hours-worked estimates is not always clear. In particular, statistical establishment and household surveys are difficult to use because of their varying quality of hours-worked estimates and their varying degree of international comparability.

GDP per capita is a very rough measure of average living standards or economic well-being and is one of the core indicators of economic performance.{{sfn|OECD|2008|loc=14}} Maximizing GDP, in principle, also allows maximizing capital usage. For this reason, GDP is systematically biased in favour of capital intensive production at the expense of knowledge and labour-intensive production. The use of capital in the GDP-measure is considered to be as valuable as the production's ability to pay taxes, profits and labor compensation. The bias of the GDP is actually the difference between the GDP and the producer income.{{sfn|Saari|2011|loc=10, 16}} 

Another labour productivity measure, output per worker, is often seen as a proper measure of labour productivity, as here: "Productivity isn't everything, but in the long run it is almost everything. A country's ability to improve its standard of living over time depends almost entirely on its ability to raise its output per worker."<ref name=Krugman1994>{{cite book|author=Paul Krugman|title=The Age of Diminishing Expectations|publisher=MIT Press|year=1994|author-link=Paul Krugman}}</ref>

[[Decoupling of wages from productivity|Productivity can decouple from wages]].<ref name="h850">{{cite report | last1=Greenspon | first1=Jacob | last2=Stansbury | first2=Anna | last3=Summers | first3=Lawrence | title=Productivity and Pay in the US and Canada | publisher=National Bureau of Economic Research | publication-place=Cambridge, MA | year=2021 | doi=10.3386/w29548 | page=}}</ref>

==Multi-factor productivity==
{{Main|Total factor productivity}}
[[File:U.S. productivity contributions 1987-2014.png|thumb|300px|right|Trends in U.S. productivity from labor, capital and multi-factor sources over the 1987–2014 period]]
When multiple inputs are considered, the measure is called multi-factor productivity or MFP and is typically estimated using [[growth accounting]].<ref name="BLS">{{cite web|title=Multifactor Productivity—Overview|url=https://www.bls.gov/mfp/mprover.htm|website=Bureau of Labor Statistics|publisher=U.S. Department of Labor|access-date=11 March 2017}}</ref> If the inputs specifically are labor and capital, and the outputs are [[value added]] intermediate outputs, the measure is called total factor productivity (TFP].<ref>Ivan Png: {{YouTube|h4FupvFDyZQ|Total Factor Productivity: Estimation}}</ref> TFP measures the residual growth that cannot be explained by the rate of change in the services of labour and capital. MFP replaced the term TFP used in the earlier literature, and both terms continue in use (usually interchangeably).{{sfn|Hulten|2009|loc=7}}

TFP is often interpreted as a rough average measure of productivity, more specifically the contribution to economic growth made by factors such as technical and organisational innovation.{{sfn|OECD|2008|loc=11}} The most famous description is that of [[Robert Solow]]'s (1957): "I am using the phrase 'technical change' as a shorthand expression for any kind of shift in the [[production function]]. Thus slowdowns, speed ups, improvements in the education of the labor force and all sorts of things will appear as 'technical change' ." The original MFP model{{sfn|Solow|1957}} involves several assumptions: that there is a stable functional relation between inputs and output at the economy-wide level of aggregation, that this function has neoclassical smoothness and curvature properties, that inputs are paid the value of their marginal product, that the function exhibits constant returns to scale, and that technical change has the Hicks'n neutral form.{{sfn|Hulten|2009|loc=5}} In practice, TFP is "a measure of our ignorance", as {{harvtxt|Abramovitz|1956}} put it, precisely because it is a residual. This ignorance covers many components, some wanted (like the effects of technical and organizational innovation), others unwanted (measurement error, omitted variables, aggregation bias, model misspecification){{sfn|Hulten|2000|loc=11}} Hence the relationship between TFP and productivity remains unclear.<ref name="assets.cambridge.org"/>

== Total productivity ==
{{main|Production (economics)}}
When all outputs and inputs are included in the productivity measure it is called total productivity. A valid measurement of total productivity necessitates considering all production inputs. If we omit an input in productivity (or income accounting) this means that the omitted input can be used unlimitedly in production without any impact on accounting results. Because total productivity includes all production inputs, it is used as an integrated variable when we want to explain income formation of the production process.

Davis has considered<ref>Davis, 1955</ref> the phenomenon of productivity, measurement of productivity, distribution of productivity gains, and how to measure such gains. He refers to an article<ref>1947, Journal of Accountancy, Feb. p.&nbsp;94</ref> suggesting that the measurement of productivity shall be developed so that it "will indicate increases or decreases in the productivity of the company and also the distribution of the 'fruits of production' among all parties at interest". According to Davis, the price system is a mechanism through which productivity gains are distributed, and besides the business enterprise, receiving parties may consist of its customers, staff and the suppliers of production inputs.

In the main article is presented the role of total productivity as a variable when explaining how income formation of production is always a balance between income generation and income distribution. The income change created by production function is always distributed to the stakeholders as economic values within the review period.

==Benefits of productivity growth==
[[File:ABS-5206.0-AustralianNationalAccounts-NationalIncomeExpenditureProduct-KeyNationalAccountsAggregates-GdpPerHourWorked-Index-A2304192L.svg|thumb|right|300px|Labour productivity growth in Australia since 1978, measured by GDP per hour worked (indexed)]]
Productivity growth is a crucial source of growth in living standards. Productivity growth means more value is added in production and this means more income is available to be distributed.<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Torchi |first=Calcagno |date=2026 |title=The Infinite Debt Problem: How to Replace Perpetual Growth with Productive Prosperity |url=https://zenodo.org/records/21970251 |journal=Zenodo}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=OECD Compendium of Productivity Indicators |url=https://www.oecd.org/en/publications/oecd-compendium-of-productivity-indicators_22252126.html |access-date=2026-08-06 |website=OECD |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Productivity Home Page : U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics |url=https://www.bls.gov/productivity/ |access-date=2026-08-06 |website=Bureau of Labor Statistics |language=en}}</ref>

At a firm or industry level, the benefits of productivity growth can be distributed in a number of different ways:
* to the workforce through higher wages and improved conditions;
* to owners of capital through higher profits and returns;
* to consumers through lower prices for goods and services;
* to the environment when efficiency gains reduce resource use or pollution per unit of output; and
* to governments through higher tax revenues on expanded economic activity.

In competitive markets these gains are transmitted largely through the price system: lower unit costs allow firms to reduce prices, raise real wages, or increase returns to capital while remaining competitive.<ref>{{Cite book |last=Joseph A Schumpeter |url=http://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.190072 |title=Capitalism Socialism And Democracy |date=1942}}</ref><ref>{{Cite journal |last=Ahn |first=Sanghoon |date=2002-01-16 |title=Competition, Innovation and Productivity Growth: A Review of Theory and Evidence |url=https://www.oecd.org/en/publications/competition-innovation-and-productivity-growth_182144868160.html |journal=OECD Economics Department Working Papers |language=en |volume=2002 |issue=3 |doi=10.1787/182144868160|url-access=subscription }}</ref> Productivity growth enables firms to meet obligations to workers, shareholders, and governments while improving or maintaining their competitive position. Simply adding more inputs without productivity gains does not raise income earned per unit of input and is likely to put downward pressure on average wages and rates of return. With productivity growth, the same resources generate more output and income, attracting additional resources into productive use.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2004-07-16 |title=The Productivity and Jobs Connection: The Long and the Short Run of It - San Francisco Fed |url=https://www.frbsf.org/research-and-insights/publications/economic-letter/2004/07/the-productivity-and-jobs-connection-the-long-and-the-short-run-of-it/ |access-date=2026-08-06 |language=en-US}}</ref>

==Drivers of productivity growth==
{{See also|Productivity-improving technologies}}
In the most immediate sense, productivity is determined by the available technology or know-how for converting resources into outputs, and the way in which resources are organized to produce goods and services. Historically, productivity has improved through [[Sociocultural evolution|evolution]] as processes with poor productivity performance are replaced with newer processes. Process improvements may include [[organizational structure]]s (e.g. core functions and supplier relationships), management systems, work arrangements, manufacturing techniques, and changing market structure. A famous example is the [[assembly line]] and the process of [[mass production]] that appeared in the decade following commercial introduction of the automobile.<ref name="Field 2004">{{cite book |doi=10.1017/CBO9780511616464.006 |chapter=Technical change and US economic growth: The interwar period and the 1990s |title=The Global Economy in the 1990s |pages=89–117 |year=2006 |last1=Field |first1=Alexander J. |editor2-first=Gianni |editor2-last=Toniolo |editor1-first=Paul W |editor1-last=Rhode |isbn=978-0-511-61646-4 |s2cid=155395606 |chapter-url=https://scholarcommons.scu.edu/econ/112 }}</ref>

Mass production dramatically reduced the labor in producing parts for and assembling the automobile, but after its widespread adoption productivity gains in automobile production were much lower. A similar pattern was observed with [[electrification]], which saw the highest productivity gains in the early decades after introduction. Many other industries show similar patterns. The pattern was again followed by the computer, information and communications industries in the late 1990s when much of the national productivity gains occurred in these industries.<ref name=egtp>{{cite web |last1=Ayres |last2=Warr |first1=Robert U. |first2=Benjamin |title=Economic growth, technological progress and energy use in the U.S. over the last century: Identifying common trends and structural change in macroeconomic time series |publisher=INSEAD Business in Society Centre |year=2006 |url=https://sites.insead.edu/facultyresearch/research/doc.cfm?did=2156 }}</ref>

There is a general understanding of the main determinants or drivers of productivity growth. Certain factors are critical for determining productivity growth. [[Office for National Statistics|The Office for National Statistics]] (UK) identifies five drivers that interact to underlie long-term productivity performance: investment, innovation, skills, enterprise and [[competition]].{{sfn|ONS|loc=ch. 3, p. 20}}
* ''Investment'' is in physical capital&nbsp;— machinery, equipment and buildings. The more capital workers have at their disposal, generally the better they are able to do their jobs, producing more and better quality output.
* ''Innovation'' is the successful exploitation of [[Learning effect (economics)|new ideas]]. New ideas can take the form of new technologies, new products or new corporate structures and ways of working. Speeding up the [[diffusion of innovations]] can boost productivity.
* ''Skills'' are defined as the quantity and quality of labour of different types available in an economy. Skills complement physical capital, and are needed to take advantage of investment in new technologies and organisational structures.
* ''Enterprise'' is defined as the seizing of new business opportunities by both start-ups and existing firms. New enterprises compete with existing firms by new ideas and technologies increasing competition. [[Entrepreneurship|Entrepreneurs]] are able to combine factors of production and new technologies forcing existing firms to adapt or exit the market.
* ''Competition'' improves productivity by creating incentives to innovate and ensures that resources are allocated to the most efficient firms. It also forces existing firms to organise work more effectively through imitations of organisational structures and technology.
In addition to the factors above, the quality of economic institutions has been linked to productivity growth. Peer-reviewed research finds that higher levels of economic freedom, particularly institutions that secure property rights, facilitate voluntary exchange, and lower barriers to entry and competition, are positively associated with total factor productivity growth, as these conditions enable entrepreneurial experimentation and more efficient resource allocation.<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Bjørnskov |first=Christian |last2=Foss |first2=Nicolai |date=2013 |title=How Strategic Entrepreneurship and The Institutional Context Drive Economic Growth |url=https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1002/sej.1148 |journal=Strategic Entrepreneurship Journal |language=en |volume=7 |issue=1 |pages=50–69 |doi=10.1002/sej.1148 |issn=1932-443X|url-access=subscription }}</ref><ref>{{Cite journal |last=Borović |first=Zoran |last2=Gligorić |first2=Dragan |last3=Trivić |first3=Jelena |date=2020-12-11 |title=Total Factor Productivity and Economic Freedom in Former Socialistic Countries |url=https://library.ien.bg.ac.rs/index.php/ea/article/view/1209 |journal=Economic Analysis |language=en |volume=53 |issue=2 |pages=95–108 |doi=10.28934/ea.20.53.2.pp95-108 |issn=2560-3949|doi-access=free }}</ref><ref>{{Cite journal |last=Liu |first=Yu-Qi |last2=Feng |first2=Chao |date=2023-04-09 |title=How Do Economic Freedom and Technological Innovation Affect Green Total-Factor Productivity? Cross-Country Evidence |url=https://doi.org/10.1080/1540496X.2022.2138325 |journal=Emerging Markets Finance and Trade |volume=59 |issue=5 |pages=1426–1443 |doi=10.1080/1540496X.2022.2138325 |issn=1540-496X|url-access=subscription }}</ref>

[[Research and development]] (R&D) tends to increase productivity growth,<ref name="n379">{{cite journal | last1=Guellec | first1=Dominique | last2=van Pottelsberghe de la Potterie | first2=Bruno | title=R&D and Productivity Growth: Panel Data Analysis of 16 OECD Countries | journal=OECD Economic Studies | volume=2001 | issue=2 | date=7 May 2003 | doi=10.1787/eco_studies-v2001-art12-en | pages=103–126}}</ref> with public R&D showing larger spillovers and smaller firms experiencing larger productivity gains from public R&D.<ref name="c871">{{cite web | title=Public R&D Spillovers and Productivity Growth, Arnaud Dyevre, Department of Economics, LSE | url=https://www.ecb.europa.eu/press/conferences/ecbforum/shared/pdf/2024/EFCB_2024_Dyevre_paper.it.pdf | access-date=26 July 2024}}</ref> [[Red tape]] and [[compliance cost]]s decrease productivity.<ref name="i280">{{cite journal | last1=Tomasi | first1=Chiara | last2=Pieri | first2=Fabio | last3=Cecco | first3=Valentina | title=Red tape and industry dynamics: a cross-country analysis | journal=Journal of Industrial and Business Economics | volume=50 | issue=2 | date=2023 | issn=0391-2078 | doi=10.1007/s40812-023-00266-0 | pages=283–320 | url=https://link.springer.com/10.1007/s40812-023-00266-0 | access-date=2026-09-01}}</ref>

See also [[List of countries by labour productivity#Productivity growth|list of countries by productivity growth]].

== Individual and team productivity ==
{{See also|Programming productivity}}
<!-- note: text goes roughly from smallest unit (individual activities) to larger units (daily activities, teams, businesses, nations) -->
Technology has enabled massive personal productivity gains. Use of computers, spreadsheets, email, and other technological advances have made it possible for a knowledge worker to seemingly produce more in a day than was previously possible in a year.<ref name=Fuller>{{cite news|title=The Paradox of Workplace Productivity|first=Ryan|last=Fuller|date=19 April 2016|url=https://hbr.org/2016/04/the-paradox-of-workplace-productivity}}</ref> Environmental factors such as [[sleep]] and [[leisure]] play a significant role in work productivity and received wage.<ref>{{cite journal|last1=Gibson|first1=Matthew|last2=Shrader|first2=Jeffrey|title=Time Use and Productivity: The Wage Returns to Sleep|journal=EScholarship|date=14 July 2014|url=http://escholarship.org/uc/item/8zp518hc|access-date=11 March 2017|language=en}}</ref>

Productivity is influenced by effective supervision and job satisfaction. An effective or knowledgeable supervisor (for example a supervisor who uses the [[Management by objectives]] method) has an easier time motivating their employees to produce more in quantity and quality. An employee who has an effective supervisor, motivating them to be more productive is likely to experience a new level of job satisfaction thereby becoming a driver of productivity itself.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Thompson |first1=K. R. |last2=Luthans |first2=Fred |first3=Willbann D. |last3=Terpening |display-authors=1 |title=The Effects of MBO on Performance and Satisfaction in a Public Sector Organization |journal=Journal of Management |volume=7 |issue=1 |pages=53–68 |year=1981 |doi=10.1177/014920638100700105 |s2cid=18992717 |url=http://digitalcommons.unl.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1021&context=leadershipfacpub }}</ref> There is also considerable evidence to support improved productivity through operant conditioning reinforcement,<ref>{{cite book |last1=Skinner |first1=B.F. |title=About Behaviorism |date=1974 |publisher=Knopf |isbn=0-394-49201-3 |url-access=registration |url=https://archive.org/details/aboutbehaviorism00skin }}</ref> successful gamification engagement,<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Robson |first1=Karen |last2=Plangger |first2=Kirk |last3=Kietzmann |first3=Jan H. |last4=McCarthy |first4=Ian |last5=Pitt |first5=Leyland |title=Game on: Engaging customers and employees through gamification |journal=Business Horizons |date=January 2016 |volume=59 |issue=1 |pages=29–36 |doi=10.1016/j.bushor.2015.08.002 |s2cid=32203003 }}</ref> and research-based recommendations on principles and implementation guidelines for using monetary rewards effectively.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Aguinis |first1=Herman |last2=Joo |first2=Harry |last3=Gottfredson |first3=Ryan K. |title=What monetary rewards can and cannot do: How to show employees the money |journal=Business Horizons |date=March 2013 |volume=56 |issue=2 |pages=241–249 |doi=10.1016/j.bushor.2012.11.007 }}</ref>

=== Detrimental impact of bullying, incivility, toxicity and psychopathy ===
[[Workplace bullying]] results in a loss of productivity, as measured by self-rated [[job performance]].<ref name=Organisational>Helge H, Sheehan MJ, Cooper CL, Einarsen S "Organisational Effects of Workplace Bullying" in Bullying and Harassment in the Workplace: Developments in Theory, Research, and Practice (2010)</ref> Over time, targets of bullying will spend more time protecting themselves against harassment by bullies and less time fulfilling their duties.<ref>{{cite thesis |last=Fisher-Blando |first=J. L. |year=2008 |title=Workplace bullying: Aggressive behavior and its effect on job satisfaction and productivity |type=PhD |publisher=University of Phoenix |url=https://www.workplaceviolence911.com/docs/20081215.pdf }}</ref> [[Workplace incivility]] has also been associated with diminished productivity in terms of quality and quantity of work.<ref name=techjournal>{{cite web|url=http://www.techjournal.org/2011/01/9-signs-your-work-place-needs-civility-6-steps-to-achieve-it/|title=9 signs your work place needs civility, 6 steps to achieve it |work=TechJournal|access-date=25 September 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150609075301/http://www.techjournal.org/2011/01/9-signs-your-work-place-needs-civility-6-steps-to-achieve-it/|archive-date=9 June 2015|url-status=dead|df=dmy-all}}</ref>

A [[toxic workplace]] is a workplace that is marked by significant drama and infighting, where personal battles often harm productivity.<ref>Benoit, Suzanne (2011) "Toxic Employees: great companies resolve this problem, you can too!" Falmouth, Maine: BCSPublishing</ref> While employees are [[Distraction|distracted]] by this, they cannot devote time and attention to the achievement of business goals.<ref>Bitting, Robert. "Using Effective Leadership Strategies in the Workplace". http://www.robertbitting.com/files/articles/Toxic-Employees-in-the-Work-Place.pdf {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110813050332/http://www.robertbitting.com/files/articles/Toxic-Employees-in-the-Work-Place.pdf |date=2011-08-13 }}. Retrieved May 13, 2011.</ref> When toxic employees leave the workplace, it can improve the [[Organizational culture|culture]] overall because the remaining staff become more engaged and productive.<ref>Mayhew, Ruth "Is employee turnover always bad?" accessed at: http://smallbusiness.chron.com/employee-turnover-always-bad-11089.html</ref> The presence of a [[workplace psychopath]] may have a serious detrimental impact on productivity in an organisation.<ref>Boddy, C. R. (2010) 'Corporate Psychopaths and Productivity', Management Services Spring, 26–30.</ref>

In companies where the [[Hierarchical organization|traditional hierarchy]] has been removed in favor of an egalitarian, team-based setup, the employees are often happier, and individual productivity is improved (as they themselves are better placed to increase the efficiency of the workfloor). Companies that have these hierarchies removed and have their employees work more in teams are called [[liberated companies]] or "Freedom Inc.'s".<ref>{{cite web|url=http://innovationexcellence.com/blog/2015/11/10/innovation-management-are-start-ups-freedom-inc-companies/|title=Innovation Management : Are start-ups Freedom Inc. companies? |publisher=Innovation Excellence}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |url=http://startups-paysdelaloire.fr/lentreprise-liberee/ |title=L'entreprise liberée |access-date=2016-07-15 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160820005708/http://startups-paysdelaloire.fr/lentreprise-liberee/ |archive-date=2016-08-20 |url-status=dead }}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.holacracy.org/how-it-works/|title=What is Holacracy|website=holacracy.org|date=2 January 2023 }}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.groupe-poult.com/a-liberated-company-turned-towards-its-ecosystem/|title=Liberated company with an innovative managerial culture |publisher=Groupe Poult}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.semco.com.br/en/download/media-coverage/bizEd-february-2004.pdf|title=SEMCO|website=semco.com.br}}</ref> The [[Kaizen]] system of bottom-up, continuous improvement was first practiced by Japanese manufacturers after World War II, most notably as part of [[The Toyota Way]].

== Business productivity ==
Productivity is one of the main concerns of business management and engineering. Many companies have formal programs for continuously improving productivity, such as a [[production assurance]] program. Whether they have a formal program or not, companies are constantly looking for ways to improve quality, reduce downtime and inputs of labor, materials, energy and purchased services. Often simple changes to operating methods or processes increase productivity, but the biggest gains are normally from adopting new technologies, which may require capital expenditures for new equipment, computers or software. Modern productivity science owes much to formal investigations that are associated with [[scientific management]].<ref name=nelson /> Although from an individual management perspective, employees may be doing their jobs well and with high levels of individual productivity, from an organizational perspective their productivity may in fact be zero or effectively negative if they are dedicated to redundant or value destroying activities.<ref name=Fuller/> In office buildings and service-centred companies, productivity is largely influenced and affected by operational byproducts—meetings.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://hbr.org/2017/07/stop-the-meeting-madness|title=Stop the Meeting Madness|date=2017-07-01|work=Harvard Business Review|access-date=2018-06-27}}</ref> The past few years have seen a positive uptick in the number of software solutions focused on improving office productivity.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://dazeinfo.com/2014/05/12/business-productivity-apps-market-worth-58-billion-2016-courtesy-byod-culture/|title=Business and Productivity Apps Market Worth $58 Billion by 2016 [Report]|date=2014-05-12|work=Dazeinfo|access-date=2018-06-27}}</ref> In truth, proper planning and procedures are more likely to help than anything else.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.yarooms.com/blog/meeting-room-productivity|title=Meeting Room Productivity / Blog / YArooms|website=www.yarooms.com|access-date=2018-06-27}}</ref>

==Productivity paradox==
{{main|Productivity paradox}}
Overall productivity growth in the US was relatively slow from the 1970s through the early 1990s,<ref name="Brynjolfsson1993">{{cite journal|last=Brynjolfsson|first=Erik|year=1993|title=The productivity paradox of information technology|journal=Communications of the ACM|volume=36|issue=12|pages=66–77|issn=0001-0782|doi=10.1145/163298.163309|s2cid=15074120|doi-access=free}}</ref> and again from the 2000s to 2020s. Although several possible causes for the slowdown have been proposed there is no consensus. The matter is subject to a continuing debate that has grown beyond questioning whether just computers can significantly increase productivity to whether the potential to increase productivity is becoming exhausted.<ref>{{cite journal|url=https://ideas.repec.org/a/ijb/journl/v13y2014i2p115-126.html|title=Testing Significance of Contributions in Growth Accounting, with Application to Testing ICT Impact on Labor Productivity of Developed Countries|first=Valentin|last=Zelenyuk|date=30 October 2018|journal=International Journal of Business and Economics|volume=13|issue=2|pages=115–126|via=ideas.repec.org}}</ref>

==National productivity==
In order to measure the productivity of a nation or an industry, it is necessary to [[Operationalization|operationalize]] the same concept of productivity as in a production unit or a company, yet, the object of modelling is substantially wider and the information more aggregate. The calculations of productivity of a nation or an industry are based on the time series of the SNA, [[System of National Accounts]]. National accounting is a system based on the recommendations of the UN (SNA 93) to measure the total production and total income of a nation and how they are used.{{sfn|Saari|2006|loc=9}}

International or national productivity growth stems from a complex interaction of factors. Some of the most important immediate factors include [[technological change]], organizational change, industry restructuring and resource reallocation, as well as [[economies of scale]] and scope. A nation's average productivity level can also be affected by the movement of resources from low-productivity to high-productivity industries and activities. Over time, other factors such as research and development and innovative effort, the development of [[human capital]] through education, and incentives from stronger competition promote the search for productivity improvements and the ability to achieve them. Ultimately, many policy, institutional and cultural factors determine a nation's success in improving productivity.

At the national level, productivity growth raises living standards because more real income improves people's ability to purchase goods and services (whether they are necessities or luxuries), enjoy leisure, improve housing and education and contribute to social and environmental programs. Some have suggested that the UK's 'productivity puzzle' is an urgent issue for policy makers and businesses to address in order to sustain growth.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://medium.com/@tausif000/the-uk-productivity-puzzle-can-manufacturing-automation-be-one-of-the-solutions-861a0758eecb|title=The UK productivity puzzle: Can manufacturing automation be one of the solutions?|last=Bordoloi|first=Tausif|date=2019-10-11|website=Medium|language=en|access-date=2019-12-03}}</ref> Over long periods of time, small differences in rates of productivity growth compound, like interest in a bank account, and can make an enormous difference to a society's prosperity. Nothing contributes more to reduction of poverty, to increases in leisure, and to the country's ability to finance education, public health, environment and the arts'.<ref>Alan Blinder and William Baumol 1993, Economics: Principles and Policy, Harcourt Brace Jovanovich, San Diego, p.&nbsp;778.</ref>

Productivity is considered basic statistical information for many international comparisons and country performance assessments and there is strong interest in comparing them internationally. The OECD<ref>{{Cite web | url=http://stats.oecd.org/Index.aspx?DataSetCode=LEVEL | title=Labour productivity levels in the total economy}}</ref> publishes an annual Compendium of Productivity Indicators<ref>{{Cite web |title=OECD Compendium of Productivity Indicators |url=https://www.oecd-ilibrary.org/industry-and-services/oecd-compendium-of-productivity-indicators_22252126 |access-date=2022-09-17 |website=www.oecd-ilibrary.org |language=en}}</ref> that includes both labor and multi-factor measures of productivity.

